12:29.740 --> 12:32.240
So how does Flyxion actually solve the problem of time?

12:32.240 --> 12:38.260
You've identified the crucial missing piece, which is the sole focus of the second monograph in our stack,

12:38.720 --> 12:40.340
adjunction and irreversibility.

12:40.840 --> 12:44.420
The problem of the arrow of time has plagued physics for over a century.

12:44.940 --> 12:47.480
Because the basic equations work backward and forward.

12:47.900 --> 12:48.400
Exactly.

12:49.220 --> 12:54.000
Classical thermodynamics tells us that entropy always increases in a closed system.

12:54.880 --> 12:56.780
The burned toast cannot unburn.

12:57.620 --> 13:01.220
But as Flyxion points out, that is merely a phenomenological gloss.

13:01.220 --> 13:02.920
Meaning it's just an observation.

13:03.120 --> 13:06.600
We see it happening, so we write it down as a rule, but we don't know why.

13:06.780 --> 13:07.220
Precisely.

13:07.360 --> 13:09.700
It's an observation, not a structural cause.

13:10.720 --> 13:15.140
Thermodynamics doesn't tell us why the asymmetry exists at a fundamental geometric level.

13:15.600 --> 13:19.360
Especially when you try to apply this to cognitive or semantic systems.

13:19.660 --> 13:19.960
Right.

13:20.020 --> 13:22.640
Because in a brain, heat isn't thermal agitation.

13:22.940 --> 13:23.340
Exactly.

13:23.520 --> 13:26.040
It's a structured incompatibility between ideas.

13:26.480 --> 13:30.140
So why does a thought process or a universe only move forward?

13:30.140 --> 13:40.040
Flyxion proposes a structural theorem for irreversibility based on the transition from a continuous field space, or RSVP plenum, to a discrete history space.

13:40.160 --> 13:42.980
Okay, what does that transition look like, continuous to discrete?

13:43.280 --> 13:51.860
Think about taking a perfectly continuous, unbroken line drawn on a piece of paper and trying to represent that line by placing a series of discrete, separate dots over it.

13:51.920 --> 13:53.720
Like digitizing an old vinyl record.

13:53.720 --> 13:54.580
Great analogy.

13:55.180 --> 14:00.580
When you digitize or discretize something continuous, information is fundamentally lost.

14:01.020 --> 14:03.920
Unless the mathematical mapping is perfectly injective.

14:04.060 --> 14:04.780
Injective.

14:04.940 --> 14:10.180
In mathematics, an injective function means there is a perfect, flawless one-to-one match.

14:11.000 --> 14:15.740
No two distinct points in the continuous field map to the exact same discrete dot.

14:15.740 --> 14:18.760
But the universe is not perfectly injective.

14:19.380 --> 14:23.060
I'm trying to think of a real-world example of this discretization process.

14:23.420 --> 14:26.260
Because dots on a line is still a bit abstract for me.

14:26.520 --> 14:30.140
Think about a complex physical process that breaks down a hole into parts.

14:30.240 --> 14:32.580
Okay, like a meat grinder.

14:32.760 --> 14:33.260
Let's follow that.

14:33.580 --> 14:39.040
If I have a cow, which is a continuous, highly complex, structured biological system,

14:39.040 --> 14:43.660
and I put that cow through an industrial meat grinder, I get a hamburger patty out the other side.

14:43.840 --> 14:45.180
A very grim picture, but yes.

14:45.260 --> 14:47.000
The patty is a discrete event.

14:47.160 --> 14:53.760
Now, mathematically, I suppose you could map the journey of every single cut of beef, every cell, as it goes through the blades and ends up in the patty.

14:53.840 --> 14:54.240
You could.

14:54.500 --> 14:57.040
The forward equation is theoretically calculable.

14:57.060 --> 14:58.320
But the irreversibility.

14:58.540 --> 15:01.780
I could never run the hamburger backward through the grinder and get the cow.

15:01.780 --> 15:07.740
The information about the cow's original cellular structure, the way the muscles connected to the bones,

15:07.920 --> 15:11.480
it's all obliterated by the process of turning it into a discrete patty.

15:11.660 --> 15:14.140
You just can't reconstruct the cow from the burger.

15:14.340 --> 15:16.520
That's a brilliantly visceral analogy.

15:16.660 --> 15:18.860
And it perfectly encapsulates the math.

15:19.080 --> 15:23.560
Flyxion defines irreversibility precisely as this reconstruction defect.

15:23.840 --> 15:24.040
Yeah.

15:24.340 --> 15:30.840
The universe operates as a continuous field, but events, the things that actually happen to get recorded, are discrete.

15:30.840 --> 15:32.340
They're the hamburger patties.

15:32.500 --> 15:32.660
Yes.

15:32.960 --> 15:37.700
When the continuous RSVP field is discretized into events, you generate a history.

15:38.400 --> 15:45.380
And because the mapping is not perfectly injective, you cannot perfectly invert the map from that discrete history back to the continuous state.

15:45.740 --> 15:46.500
The cow is gone.

15:46.720 --> 15:49.960
So the arrow of time isn't like a force pushing us forward.

15:50.100 --> 15:53.500
Time is just the impossibility of going backward because the data has been destroyed.

15:53.820 --> 15:54.260
Exactly.

15:54.700 --> 15:58.280
The text states that the entropy defect isn't thermal heat.

15:58.280 --> 16:01.940
It is the measure-theoretic shadow of that lost information.

16:03.100 --> 16:09.660
To prove this, Flyxion utilizes a highly complex mathematical tool called the Jensen-Shannon divergence.

16:10.100 --> 16:12.280
I saw that term in the papers and immediately glazed over.

16:12.440 --> 16:14.040
What is a Jensen-Shannon divergence?

16:14.620 --> 16:18.700
It is a way of measuring the similarity between two probability distributions.

16:19.580 --> 16:24.820
Imagine two slightly different paths the system could take that end up at the exact same final state.

16:24.920 --> 16:25.160
Okay.

16:25.160 --> 16:35.420
The Jensen-Shannon divergence measures exactly how much distinct information is destroyed when the universe blurs the distinction between those two paths and just records the final destination.

16:35.820 --> 16:41.480
The higher the divergence, the more information is lost and the more violently irreversible the process is.

16:41.760 --> 16:46.980
The arrow of time is literally generated by the universe failing to keep track of its own continuous nuances.

16:46.980 --> 16:56.860
And the mechanism doing the grinding, the thing that is actually chopping the continuous reality into a discrete history, is a mathematical operator Flyxion calls refuse.

16:57.580 --> 16:57.880
Yes.

16:58.440 --> 17:02.420
This comes from the SpherePop calculus we mentioned earlier regarding institutions.

17:02.420 --> 17:07.980
The SpherePop calculus has several event operators that govern how systems interact.

17:08.740 --> 17:13.500
There's POP for creation, bind for connection, collapse for destruction.

17:13.980 --> 17:17.220
But the refuse operator is the critical engine for time.

17:17.580 --> 17:18.160
Oh, so.

17:18.580 --> 17:23.300
